over the weekend bitcoin crashed  aggressively there's a lot of people  wondering what was going on in order to  better understand what happened you need  to understand the structural differences  between the new market and the old  legacy financial market the new market  bitcoin and cryptocurrencies it trades  24 7 365. it's always on but the legacy  financial system has hours of operations  it's not open over the weekend and of  course on a weekend like this it's a  three-day weekend because monday serves  as a bank holiday and so going into the  weekend everyone knew that for three  days you would not be able to move fiat  currencies from your bank into a crypto  exchange to be able to buy any more  bitcoin or cryptocurrencies this means  that there is no buyer of last resort it  means that that persistent bid that  inflow of new capital as prices drop  wasn't going to be there and so  naturally as the bitcoin price started  to fall and there were no new buyers  that meant that the price was only going  to continue in free fall and so as  bitcoin went down and down and down it  started to trigger liquidations and  that's where we saw a cascading effect  liquidation after liquidation after  liquidation that led to not only for  sellers but also panic and fear in the  market  now that we're on the other side of the  weekend we see that over seven billion  dollars of losses were captured over the  last three days if we go ahead and we  take a look here this coindesk article  looked at the glass node data and it  shows that approximately 555 000 bitcoin  changed hands between eighteen thousand  and twenty three thousand dollar price  of bitcoin which served both as a strong  support and a resistance but the losses  are ranging between one point five  billion and two billion dollars every  single day and if you look at the  historical comparison on the chart you  can see a massive spike in all of the  losses that investors were taking  all the way here on the far right you  can see that it was the highest number  of realized loss ever in history and so  of course yes plenty of investors were  simply selling because they wanted to  they were fearful of what could happen  but many of them actually were for  sellers they were being liquidated  because they had too much leverage in  the system  so naturally as you begin to look at  this past weekend and you realize there  was seven billion dollars of losses  understand that a lot of it had to do  with the market structure difference  between the new world and the old world  if more people weren't able to take fiat  currencies and get it into the crypto  systems get it onto those exchanges and  be able to buy these lower prices it  meant that bitcoin was going to continue  to fall until eventually either all the  liquidations had already happened or  there was an exhaustion of the sellers  we saw bitcoin trade all the way down in  the 17 000s we're back up above 20 000  now no one has a crystal ball no one  knows what the future holds but what we  do know is that whenever you see a long  weekend like what's coming up in july  4th you've got to understand that there  are no new funds to flow into the crypto  system and it's likely to have  significant volatility during those  times this was a three-day weekend over  july 4th as somebody like arthur hayes  recently pointed out it's a five-day  break  so of course you should understand the  difference in market structure and you  should be prepared for these moments  everyone needs to understand that when  no new funds can flow from the old  system into the new system that  volatility almost always happens hey you  did you like this video great we make  five of them a day and post them here on  this channel make sure you subscribe  like the video and see you next time
 

